  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 60af0432a3c869e48a591344a8dafd71, type: 3}
  m_Name: PrePhys 3
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  question: 'While reviewing animal study results for a new chemotherapy, the research
    team asks for your input on possible side effects. You point out that some side
    effects people commonly experience may not appear in these animal models.


    <b>In
    this scenario, why is it important for the research team to consult a physician
    during preclinical testing?</b>'
  explanation: Physicians bring valuable insight from caring for patients, including
    how people often respond to cancer treatments and which side effects or complications
    may not show up in animal studies. This perspective helps research teams think
    ahead about safety and better understand what early results might mean for people.
    By involving physicians early, researchers can design studies that better reflect
    real patient experiences and help make the move into human trials safer and more
    thoughtful.
  correctAnswer: To highlight differences between animals and people that could affect
    how the treatment works or causes side effects
  incorrectAnswer1: To decide which patients will benefit from the new treatment
    based on the animal study results
  incorrectAnswer2: To select a different drug compound that would work in the chosen
    animal models
  incorrectAnswer3: To help the research team decide if animal studies are needed
    before human trials begin
  questionRole: Physician
  questionType: MultipleChoice
  questionStage: PreClinical
